From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 22:35:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Make MobileNet inference independent of batch composition

PoseMobileNet.extract_features calls mobilenet_v2.training_scope() with no
arguments.  That helper defaults to is_training=True, so every graph
DeepLabCut builds -- including the inference graphs built by
BasePoseNet.test() and BasePoseNet.inference() -- normalises with the
statistics of whatever images happen to share the batch.  Dumping the
graph confirms it: for all four stock net_types (mobilenet_v2_1.0, 0.75,
0.5 and 0.35) and for all three entry points (train / test / inference)
all 52 MobilenetV2 FusedBatchNormV3 nodes carry is_training=True, and the
inference graphs register 104 dangling moving-average update ops in
tf.GraphKeys.UPDATE_OPS that nothing ever runs.  The other two backbones
do not have this defect: resnet_50 builds 53 nodes and efficientnet-b0
builds 48, all with is_training=False, in all three graphs.

Unlike the MuNet decoder of the previous patch, this path is reached by a
stock project: net_type=mobilenet_v2_* is a documented option and needs no
hand-edited pose_cfg.yaml.

Measured on a mobilenet_v2_1.0 model trained for 10000 iterations on the
bundled openfield example (real ImageNet weights, DLC defaults, so
batch_size 1 and the imgaug augmenter), the prediction for a frame depends
on the rest of its batch:

    16-frame clip, deeplabcut.analyze_videos, snapshot-10000
      batchsize 1 vs batchsize 8   mean 0.576 px, p95 1.371 px, max 3.432 px
      batchsize 1 vs batchsize 4   mean 0.567 px, p95 1.481 px, max 5.054 px

    same probe frame, batch size 4, only the three companions change
      three other frames of the same video      up to 1.13 px
      three copies of the probe frame itself    up to 1.53 px
      three black frames                        up to 6.55 px
      three white frames                        up to 6.77 px

Because evaluate_network forces batch_size = 1 while analyze_videos uses
the project's batch_size (8 by default), the reported evaluation error and
the keypoints written out for a video were not produced by the same
normalisation.  On this model the whole-dataset error is 3.48 px train /
4.72 px test at batch size 1 and 3.64 px / 4.62 px at batch size 8.

Fix: build the backbone one image at a time whenever the inference graph
has a static batch size larger than one, and concatenate the results.
Each image is then normalised by its own statistics, exactly as it would
be at batch size 1.

This deliberately does NOT switch inference to the moving averages, which
is what the equivalent MuNet fix does and what the arg_scope was
presumably meant to do.  That was tried first and it is badly wrong here:
the same snapshot evaluated with the stored moving averages gives 11.85 px
train / 12.79 px test, a 2.7x regression, because DeepLabCut trains this
backbone at batch_size 1 -- batch-norm in training mode is then instance
normalisation, and the accumulated averages describe the heavily augmented
training stream (global_scale 0.8, scale jitter 0.5-1.25, crops, CLAHE,
motion blur, covering, elastic transform) rather than the images inference
actually sees.  Freezing the backbone at the ImageNet statistics instead,
the way resnet.py and efficientnet.py do, is no better: retraining for
10000 iterations that way gives 9.68 px / 12.68 px.  For the record the
population statistics can be made usable, but only by recomputing them:
re-accumulating the 52 moving_mean/moving_variance pairs over the 110
unaugmented training images at inference resolution and then normalising
with them gives 3.75 px / 4.71 px and is likewise batch-independent.  That
is a change for upstream to make at the end of train(), not something a
packaging patch can do to snapshots users already have.

This does NOT change results for existing trained models.  At batch size 1
the graph is untouched and the predictions are bit-identical (0.00000 px
over every frame of every comparison).  At batch size 4 and 8 the
predictions now equal the batch-size-1 predictions exactly, so accuracy
moves to the batch-size-1 figure: over snapshots 9000, 9500 and 10000 the
RMSE against the labelled frames is 3.994 / 3.753 / 4.122 px both before
(at batch size 1) and after, against 4.179 / 4.034 / 4.316 px for the
unpatched batch-size-8 path.  Every batch-composition comparison above
becomes 0.00000 px; end to end through deeplabcut.analyze_videos the
batchsize-1 and batchsize-8 outputs agree to 2e-5 px, which is float
scheduling noise rather than batch dependence, and evaluate_network still
reports 3.48 px / 4.72 px.  The variable set of the inference graph is unchanged
(264 variables, identical names and shapes), so existing snapshots restore
as before, and the training graph is untouched -- still 52 is_training=True
nodes and 104 UPDATE_OPS, which slim.learning.create_train_op does wire in
as control dependencies of the loss (all 104 are reachable from the train
op, and the statistics really do move: mean |moving_mean| 0.493 -> 0.555
and mean moving_variance 3.180 -> 9.464 between the ImageNet checkpoint
and iteration 10000).

Cost: none at run time on CPU (721 ms vs 730 ms per batch of 8 for a
480x640 frame), because the per-image branches are independent and TF
schedules them in parallel; the backbone subgraph is built once per image
in the batch, so building the inference graph goes from 3.5 s to 25.7 s at
batch size 8.  Graph construction happens once per analyze_videos call.

Not covered: export_model sets batch_size to None, so an exported graph
keeps a dynamic batch dimension and takes the unmodified path.  Used one
frame at a time -- which is how DeepLabCut-Live drives it -- that is
already per-image normalisation; a consumer that batches an exported
MobileNet graph still sees the old behaviour.  PoseMultiNet.extract_features
carries the same training_scope() call for its mobilenet branch, but that
class is only reached when pose_cfg.yaml sets stride < 8 and no code path
writes such a value.

Not reported upstream: mobilenet.py carries this arg_scope unchanged on
all 111 remote branches of the DeepLabCut repository, including
origin/dev, origin/main and origin/backup/tf-deprecation, and no matching
issue exists.

Separately, and independently of this bug, net_type=mobilenet_v2_* cannot
be used at all out of the box: every URL in
pose_estimation_tensorflow/models/pretrained/pretrained_model_urls.yaml
for MobileNet returns HTTP 403, so create_training_dataset dies with
urllib.error.HTTPError.  See tensorflow/models#13561 (open since April
2025) and DeepLabCut#2937 / #3243.  The resnet and efficientnet URLs in
the same file are still live.
---
 deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/mobilenet.py | 17 +++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/mobilenet.py b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/mobilenet.py
index ffd34aa..707342c 100644
--- a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/mobilenet.py
+++ b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/nnets/mobilenet.py
@@ -63,11 +63,24 @@ class PoseMobileNet(BasePoseNet):
     def __init__(self, cfg):
         super().__init__(cfg)
 
-    def extract_features(self, inputs):
+    def extract_features(self, inputs, is_training=False):
         net_fun, net_arg_scope = networks[self.cfg["net_type"]]
         im_centered = self.center_inputs(inputs)
+        batch_size = im_centered.shape[0]
         with slim.arg_scope(net_arg_scope()):
-            net, end_points = net_fun(im_centered)
+            if is_training or batch_size is None or batch_size == 1:
+                net, end_points = net_fun(im_centered)
+            else:
+                nets, points = [], []
+                with tf.compat.v1.variable_scope(
+                    tf.compat.v1.get_variable_scope(), reuse=tf.compat.v1.AUTO_REUSE
+                ):
+                    for i in range(int(batch_size)):
+                        n_i, ep_i = net_fun(im_centered[i : i + 1])
+                        nets.append(n_i)
+                        points.append(ep_i)
+                net = tf.concat(nets, axis=0)
+                end_points = {k: tf.concat([p[k] for p in points], axis=0) for k in points[0]}
 
         return net, end_points
 
@@ -94,5 +107,5 @@ class PoseMobileNet(BasePoseNet):
         return out
 
     def get_net(self, inputs, is_training=False):
-        net, end_points = self.extract_features(inputs)
+        net, end_points = self.extract_features(inputs, is_training=is_training)
         return self.prediction_layers(net, end_points)
